Large blocks are kept for reuse. frigg's slab_pool maps every block
above its largest bucket (32 KiB) on its own, the block's pages, a page
for its header and the 256 KiB it is aligned in, and unmaps the whole
mapping when the block is freed, so a program that allocates and frees
a 64 KiB block for each request makes an mmap, an munmap and a page
fault for the header and for every page it touches, each time: 1.9
page faults a SELECT transaction in PostgreSQL, 7.4 a TPC-B
transaction, and 1.6 a run of a PHP compute loop, where glibc keeps
such blocks in its heap and takes none. The pool maps and unmaps large
blocks through map_large() and unmap_large() when its policy has them,
and through map() and unmap() as before when it does not or when it
maps with an alignment; the slabs' map_slab() is not among them.
mlibc's VirtualAllocator puts a mapping that is freed in one of 16
slots, if it is no longer than 4 MiB plus what frigg adds and the slots
hold no more than 16 MiB in all, and gives it to the next large block
that needs a mapping of exactly that length, without a system call. A
slot is one word, taken and filled by compare-and-swap, so that no lock
is held that a fork's child could find held by a thread it does not
have. Memory kept is not cleared, which malloc does not owe, and calloc
clears what it returns. glibc keeps freed blocks of up to 32 MiB; this
cache keeps less, to bound what an idle process holds.

